00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:29.343
- [Voiceover] Alan asks,
"Should I wait until my website

00:01:29.343 --> 00:01:32.469
"is 100% built before putting out content

00:01:32.469 --> 00:01:34.836
"or put out content
while I'm building it?"

00:01:34.836 --> 00:01:36.865
- Alan, there's dude by
the name of Tommy Mottola,

00:01:36.865 --> 00:01:38.944
Google him up, who once
said something to me

00:01:38.944 --> 00:01:40.023
in a private meeting.

00:01:40.023 --> 00:01:43.297
He said, "I never let any
of my artists go on TV

00:01:43.297 --> 00:01:44.806
"until I was selling something,"

00:01:44.806 --> 00:01:46.837
and his notion was until the CD was out,

00:01:46.837 --> 00:01:49.983
which is his world, why
would I put Mariah on TV.

00:01:49.983 --> 00:01:52.388
If she was a week early and
the girl that got inspired,

00:01:52.388 --> 00:01:56.622
or guy, couldn't run to
Virgin Megastore and get it,

00:01:56.622 --> 00:01:57.936
well that was a wasted opportunity

00:01:57.936 --> 00:01:59.375
and I'm a big believer in that.

00:01:59.375 --> 00:02:01.117
The only thing I wanted, so the answer is

00:02:01.117 --> 00:02:04.027
I would wait, but I want to
context this for everybody.

00:02:04.027 --> 00:02:06.870
My question is what's the objective.

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:08.592
If the objective is to put
out content that drives

00:02:08.592 --> 00:02:10.601
to the website but then gets people to

00:02:10.601 --> 00:02:13.614
sign up for something, buy
something, do something,

00:02:13.614 --> 00:02:16.197
if you're able to execute
the business objective

00:02:16.197 --> 00:02:19.517
outside of the website,
I would do that now

00:02:19.517 --> 00:02:21.281
because you're building
up storytelling and

00:02:21.281 --> 00:02:23.197
leverage and equity that later

00:02:23.197 --> 00:02:24.753
you can drive to your website.

00:02:24.753 --> 00:02:26.157
It feels like you're wasting time

00:02:26.157 --> 00:02:27.884
and missing the opportunity.

00:02:27.884 --> 00:02:29.779
That's a big problem I
have with a lot of people.

00:02:29.779 --> 00:02:32.252
They theoretically believe in a process

00:02:32.252 --> 00:02:34.412
when you can ...

00:02:34.412 --> 00:02:36.072
I think that a lot of brands should be

00:02:36.072 --> 00:02:38.335
monetizing their social media content

00:02:38.335 --> 00:02:40.041
and a lot of them are trying
to use social media content

00:02:40.041 --> 00:02:43.198
to drive to a website where
they're selling banner ads

00:02:43.198 --> 00:02:44.813
and I just think they
should be selling against

00:02:44.813 --> 00:02:46.170
the impressions of getting in social

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:47.437
because it's the same game.

00:02:47.437 --> 00:02:49.770
They've made a religious
and historical belief

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:51.453
that they need to sell to the dotcom.

00:02:51.453 --> 00:02:53.289
I'm worried you're doing the same thing,

00:02:53.289 --> 00:02:54.715
so my answer to you very simply is

00:02:54.715 --> 00:02:56.492
the way you're going to make your money,

00:02:56.492 --> 00:02:58.500
the reason you're doing it,
whatever you're trying to do,

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:00.591
if you're able to do that through content

00:03:00.591 --> 00:03:03.887
in its native space and
not drive to a dotcom,

00:03:03.887 --> 00:03:05.326
then do it there.

00:03:05.326 --> 00:03:06.803
Even when you have the website,

00:03:06.803 --> 00:03:09.066
I would practice doing it there.

00:03:09.066 --> 00:03:10.910
- [Voiceover] Elliott asks,
" How would you recommend

00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:13.285
"looking for a co-founder?"

00:03:13.285 --> 00:03:15.960
- Elliott, I think the best
way to look to a co-founder,

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:17.759
look for a co-founder, excuse me,

00:03:17.759 --> 00:03:21.997
is to first understand
how honest and self-aware

00:03:21.997 --> 00:03:23.796
are you with yourself.

00:03:23.796 --> 00:03:26.580
The number one thing that you
want to find in a co-founder,

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:29.890
in my opinion, is somebody
who checks the boxes

00:03:29.890 --> 00:03:33.489
and has strengths in a
space where you don't.

00:03:33.489 --> 00:03:36.949
Does she provide the black
and white to your gray?

00:03:36.949 --> 00:03:39.107
Does he provide the magic to your

00:03:39.107 --> 00:03:40.860
very kind of straightforward?

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:42.033
Is he the salesman?

00:03:42.033 --> 00:03:45.597
Does she love HR where you don't want to

00:03:45.597 --> 00:03:47.094
deal with any of the people?

00:03:47.094 --> 00:03:48.454
You need to yin and yang.

00:03:48.454 --> 00:03:49.567
I think it's very important.

00:03:49.567 --> 00:03:51.971
I'm a big believer in
doubling down on strengths,

00:03:51.971 --> 00:03:54.212
but I do think from a
co-founder standpoint

00:03:54.212 --> 00:03:56.417
you want to get the check
boxes of the core things

00:03:56.417 --> 00:03:59.749
that drive a business and so I think

00:03:59.749 --> 00:04:01.409
you're looking for that counterpart.

00:04:01.409 --> 00:04:03.592
The biggest reason so many of you

00:04:03.592 --> 00:04:05.455
are going to struggle with this

00:04:05.455 --> 00:04:08.874
is you're not willing to
be honest with yourself.

00:04:08.874 --> 00:04:12.381
You think you have magic
and you're a good salesman.

00:04:12.381 --> 00:04:15.050
Yeah, you think you can get by
with your accounting skills.

00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:17.372
You think because it
hurts your feelings to not

00:04:17.372 --> 00:04:18.648
bet on your weaknesses.

00:04:18.648 --> 00:04:20.587
I'm a big believer in my weaknesses.

00:04:20.587 --> 00:04:22.851
I would actually say, I
don't think I've said this

00:04:22.851 --> 00:04:24.929
very publicly, I've alluded to it.

00:04:24.929 --> 00:04:27.842
I say that I suck crap at 99% of things.

00:04:27.842 --> 00:04:29.572
I would actually, this is a new thought

00:04:29.572 --> 00:04:31.800
maybe because India is here
looking for more content.

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:33.379
Show India, I think she deserves it.

00:04:33.379 --> 00:04:34.689
- Hello.

00:04:36.329 --> 00:04:38.628
- You know, I think that my weaknesses

00:04:38.628 --> 00:04:41.727
are as big, if not a bigger
factor in my success.

00:04:41.727 --> 00:04:44.003
It's my willingness to
accept my weaknesses.

00:04:44.003 --> 00:04:47.125
It's my proudness of my weaknesses

00:04:47.125 --> 00:04:48.402
that has allowed me to win.

00:04:48.402 --> 00:04:49.470
Are you willing to go there?

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:52.301
Way too many of you don't want to accept

00:04:52.301 --> 00:04:54.949
your weak points, you're
struggling with it.

00:04:54.949 --> 00:04:55.867
As a matter of fact, I'm going right into

00:04:55.867 --> 00:04:56.842
the Question of the Day.

00:04:56.842 --> 00:04:58.966
I want you to list on Thanksgiving

00:04:58.966 --> 00:05:01.798
the weaknesses you have
that you're thankful for.

00:05:01.798 --> 00:05:03.563
That's how important this is.

00:05:03.563 --> 00:05:04.806
Let's move on.

00:05:04.806 --> 00:05:06.849
- Dear Gary, My name's Tim

00:05:06.849 --> 00:05:08.648
and I'm a marketing consultant.

00:05:08.648 --> 00:05:11.284
I have my own blog, but
I don't do any podcasts

00:05:11.284 --> 00:05:15.454
or videos, basically anything
that involves me speaking.

00:05:15.454 --> 00:05:17.807
That's because I have this
terrible Russian accent

00:05:17.807 --> 00:05:21.338
and I'm worried that it might
hurt my trustworthiness.

00:05:21.338 --> 00:05:23.960
Do you think this is
something I should worry about

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:27.857
or people from US don't
care about my Russian accent

00:05:27.857 --> 00:05:30.158
as long as I give them tons of value?

00:05:30.158 --> 00:05:31.362
Thanks.

00:05:31.362 --> 00:05:32.864
- Thank you, Tim.

00:05:32.864 --> 00:05:35.785
First of all, clearly I
might be dramatically biased

00:05:35.785 --> 00:05:38.142
because you sound like all my relatives.

00:05:41.054 --> 00:05:43.076
That Russian accent is so endearing to me

00:05:43.076 --> 00:05:45.134
that made me feel at home.

00:05:46.454 --> 00:05:48.241
I think the ...

00:05:48.241 --> 00:05:50.471
Look, do I think there
are certain Americans

00:05:50.471 --> 00:05:52.837
in the American market will hear that

00:05:52.837 --> 00:05:54.169
and jump to conclusions?

00:05:54.169 --> 00:05:56.071
I absolutely do, let's live in the world

00:05:56.071 --> 00:05:57.255
we actually live in.

00:05:57.255 --> 00:05:59.936
Current events in our society prove that.

00:05:59.936 --> 00:06:02.304
There's a lot of things
still grounded in our society

00:06:02.304 --> 00:06:05.637
that maybe many of us wish
that wouldn't be the case.

00:06:05.637 --> 00:06:09.236
Do I think that people look at accents as

00:06:09.236 --> 00:06:12.613
a sign of inferiority,
not as much intellect?

00:06:12.613 --> 00:06:14.541
I absolutely believe that to be true.

00:06:14.541 --> 00:06:16.921
On the flip side, I think, Tim my friend,

00:06:16.921 --> 00:06:18.035
you're looking at the negative.

00:06:18.035 --> 00:06:20.531
What about all the people
that who do have accents,

00:06:20.531 --> 00:06:22.424
by the way a crap load?

00:06:22.424 --> 00:06:24.152
Let me tell you another thing.

00:06:24.152 --> 00:06:26.382
As a marketing person, you're
speaking to entrepreneurs.

00:06:26.382 --> 00:06:27.891
Do you know the far majority

00:06:27.891 --> 00:06:30.469
of pure entrepreneurs
and who have that hustle?

00:06:30.469 --> 00:06:31.942
Many of them in this amazing country

00:06:31.942 --> 00:06:34.985
do have accents and so
I think you're looking

00:06:34.985 --> 00:06:37.629
at the negative instead of the positives.

00:06:37.629 --> 00:06:39.964
I do also believe that there's enormous,

00:06:39.964 --> 00:06:43.116
and I believe the 
VaynerNation is a big part of this,

00:06:43.116 --> 00:06:46.467
because look, the way I roll

00:06:46.467 --> 00:06:48.127
is something that a lot of people

00:06:48.127 --> 00:06:49.530
don't think is the right way to go.

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:51.749
I have too much bravado, too much cursing,

00:06:51.749 --> 00:06:53.885
too much oomph, too much all of that,

00:06:53.885 --> 00:06:57.728
and so I think it's
massively important ...

00:06:57.728 --> 00:06:59.669
(laughing)

00:06:59.669 --> 00:07:01.048
Nice work.

00:07:01.048 --> 00:07:02.244
I like watching A-time.

00:07:02.244 --> 00:07:05.454
He loves getting on the show.

00:07:05.454 --> 00:07:07.862
This time, I'm just really enjoying.

00:07:07.862 --> 00:07:09.302
I'm enjoying.

00:07:09.862 --> 00:07:11.484
He doesn't like it as much when I know

00:07:11.484 --> 00:07:13.861
which was a big victory for me.

00:07:14.661 --> 00:07:18.579
I really think that people

00:07:19.679 --> 00:07:21.138
need to recognize

00:07:21.138 --> 00:07:24.093
that there's pros and
cons with everything.

00:07:24.093 --> 00:07:27.322
My intuition is that if you
think that you can communicate

00:07:27.322 --> 00:07:30.976
through video or audio podcast,

00:07:30.976 --> 00:07:32.859
then that is something
you should invest in

00:07:32.859 --> 00:07:34.704
and that you should not
worry about the market

00:07:34.704 --> 00:07:36.376
because the market will come to you

00:07:36.376 --> 00:07:38.931
if you believe that you're
actually good at it.

00:07:38.931 --> 00:07:40.844
Unless, you're not.

00:07:40.844 --> 00:07:42.656
It comes down to are you good at it.

00:07:42.656 --> 00:07:45.013
Look, you clearly want to go there

00:07:45.013 --> 00:07:47.115
because you just made a video question

00:07:47.115 --> 00:07:48.820
and you got the exposure here.

00:07:48.820 --> 00:07:50.178
I'm sure you're probably even using

00:07:50.178 --> 00:07:52.351
The #AskGaryVee Show
as a little test case,

00:07:52.351 --> 00:07:53.987
but don't let the 
VaynerNation who's all going to

00:07:53.987 --> 00:07:55.728
jump in here and say "Do it, Tim, do it."

00:07:55.728 --> 00:07:57.271
You're part of this community and

00:07:57.271 --> 00:07:58.421
they're going to build you up.

00:07:58.421 --> 00:08:00.371
The real answer is,
once you start doing it,

00:08:00.371 --> 00:08:02.611
do people give a crap and to me,

00:08:02.611 --> 00:08:04.562
the fact that I know based on your actions

00:08:04.562 --> 00:08:06.466
that you want to do it, the bigger answer

00:08:06.466 --> 00:08:09.228
to your question is go and
do it for the next 100 days

00:08:09.228 --> 00:08:12.364
and then figure out was it a prejudice or

00:08:12.364 --> 00:08:13.803
preconceived notions that didn't allow you

00:08:13.803 --> 00:08:15.626
to have the upside and then you adjust

00:08:15.626 --> 00:08:18.064
and that's the real answer, my man.

00:08:18.064 --> 00:08:19.701
- [Voiceover] Danny asks, "In the past,

00:08:19.701 --> 00:08:20.850
"what was your equivalent of that

00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:23.532
"one almond moment from episode 46?

00:08:23.532 --> 00:08:25.471
"What did you potentially miss out on?"

00:08:25.471 --> 00:08:27.479
- Danny, I want everybody
in the VaynerNation

00:08:27.479 --> 00:08:32.052
to go to their copy of
Crush It!, my first book

00:08:32.052 --> 00:08:33.852
if you've got it, and I
want you to go to the first

00:08:33.852 --> 00:08:36.174
20 or 30 pages where I do acknowledgments.

00:08:36.174 --> 00:08:38.496
You will notice that I
acknowledge every one of

00:08:38.496 --> 00:08:41.095
my family members and then a random name.

00:08:41.095 --> 00:08:43.150
That random name is Travis Kalanik.

00:08:43.150 --> 00:08:45.972
Travis is the co-founder and
CEO of a company called Uber

00:08:45.972 --> 00:08:48.039
that is rumored today to be raising at

00:08:48.039 --> 00:08:51.225
a $40 billion valuation.

00:08:51.225 --> 00:08:55.213
When they were raising money
at a $4 million valuation,

00:08:55.213 --> 00:08:58.370
I passed even though
Travis is one of my boys

00:08:58.370 --> 00:09:00.750
and I think is one of the best
entrepreneurs in the world.

00:09:00.750 --> 00:09:03.420
I, at that moment, just
bought a new apartment

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:05.848
that liquidated me at
a very aggressive level

00:09:05.848 --> 00:09:08.238
and I wasn't so sure
about the idea of Uber

00:09:08.238 --> 00:09:10.670
that early on, but not that
I didn't like that idea.

00:09:10.670 --> 00:09:12.754
I just didn't think that
Garret, the co-founder

00:09:12.754 --> 00:09:16.655
of Stumble Upon and then Uber
who came up with the idea,

00:09:16.655 --> 00:09:18.871
and Travis, I didn't
think they were actually

00:09:18.871 --> 00:09:19.777
going to do it.

00:09:19.777 --> 00:09:21.089
I thought they were going
to hire somebody else

00:09:21.089 --> 00:09:22.854
and as somebody who went
through, and you've heard me

00:09:22.854 --> 00:09:24.874
talk about in this past
my failures where I put

00:09:24.874 --> 00:09:27.148
other CEOs in place and I don't drive it,

00:09:27.148 --> 00:09:30.631
if it was them driving
it, I would have got in.

00:09:30.631 --> 00:09:33.674
Hopefully, maybe, maybe
not, but not once but twice

00:09:33.674 --> 00:09:35.995
because Travis and I are
such boys that I pass

00:09:35.995 --> 00:09:39.094
on investing in Uber at the angel round.

00:09:39.094 --> 00:09:41.347
Now, I later invested in
them and I'm going to do

00:09:41.347 --> 00:09:44.411
quite well, but the valuation
differences are substantial

00:09:44.411 --> 00:09:47.732
and that $25,000 investment
that I probably would have made,

00:09:47.732 --> 00:09:49.890
because those were the size
of the checks that I made,

00:09:49.890 --> 00:09:52.468
right now would be worth
in the ballpark of,

00:09:52.468 --> 00:09:54.406
if at this $40 billion
valuation, I don't know what

00:09:54.406 --> 00:09:56.775
the dilution was or the
prorata, but you can

00:09:56.775 --> 00:09:59.393
very comfortably say that
you're looking at somebody

00:09:59.393 --> 00:10:01.570
who missed an almond by passing

00:10:01.570 --> 00:10:03.729
in a world where I was
writing $25,000 checks

00:10:03.729 --> 00:10:05.934
to a lot of dumb crap, let alone the guy,

00:10:05.934 --> 00:10:09.614
the only guy that I gave
a shout out to in my book

00:10:09.614 --> 00:10:12.877
in 2009, two years before Uber came out.

00:10:12.877 --> 00:10:17.149
That $25,000 investment is probably worth

00:10:17.149 --> 00:10:22.149
in the ballpark between
$75 and $200 million.

00:10:23.338 --> 00:10:26.085
That's one big (bleep) almond.

00:10:26.085 --> 00:10:28.023
Do you know, and I sit
here in front of you

00:10:28.023 --> 00:10:30.182
with ambitions to buy the New York Jets

00:10:30.182 --> 00:10:33.274
and no question if I
made that one decision,

00:10:33.274 --> 00:10:36.045
that one simple decision
that was right there

00:10:36.045 --> 00:10:38.928
for the taking for me, I would be

00:10:38.928 --> 00:10:41.130
dramatically further along to my goal

00:10:41.130 --> 00:10:43.766
right this minute than I actually am.

00:10:44.831 --> 00:10:46.779
Remember I told you
yesterday that I can take

00:10:46.779 --> 00:10:48.381
8,000 punches in the face?

00:10:48.381 --> 00:10:52.132
That's 25,000 punches in
the face in one punch.

00:10:52.132 --> 00:10:53.675
That's what you have to do
when you're playing the game.

00:10:53.675 --> 00:10:54.556
That's where you have to understand

